# Local Docker Compose / Vite defaults (copy to .env as needed)
WEB_PORT=5173
API_PORT=8000

# Browser-facing API base URL (host machine, not the Docker service name).
#
# Read at BUILD time by `apps/web/src/shared/api/config.ts`, so changing it in
# a deployed environment needs a rebuild, not a restart.
#
# Deployed, this is the API's Tailscale hostname — from
# `npm run tailnet:status`, no trailing slash:
#   VITE_API_URL=https://<machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net
# Leaving the localhost default in a deployed build points the browser at *the
# visitor's own machine*, which is a failure that looks like a working build.
#
# **Serve and Funnel share this value.** The hostname is the same either way —
# Funnel does not issue a second name, it publishes the existing one — so
# widening the transport is not a rebuild. What changes is only who can resolve
# it: measured 2026-09-04, `pc.tail80c91b.ts.net` answers on this machine's
# MagicDNS and is NXDOMAIN on 8.8.8.8, which is exactly the "Failed to fetch" a
# phone outside the tailnet reports. See `docs/deploy-plan.md`.
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Comma-separated origins allowed by the API CORS middleware.
#
# This is what lets the browser accept the API's responses. It is NOT access
# control: CORS is a browser policy sent in a response header, and `curl` never
# asks for it. What restricts access is the tailnet the API is published on and
# the passkey session every route now requires — not this line.
#
# Deployed, append the Vercel origin, keeping the local ones so `npm run dev`
# still works:
#   CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173,https://<project>.vercel.app
#
# **The `ts.net` hostname does not belong in this list, and Funnel does not
# change that.** What goes here is every origin a *page* is loaded from, and the
# `ts.net` name is the API's own origin — the destination of the request, never
# the `Origin` header on it. Adding it would allow a page nobody serves. The
# list is therefore identical under Serve and under Funnel: verified 2026-09-04,
# an `OPTIONS /api/auth/login/options` with `Origin:
# https://edicius-hq-web.vercel.app` came back 200 with a matching
# `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` over the tailnet, which is the same answer it
# will give over Funnel.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5173,http://127.0.0.1:5173

# What a passkey is bound to.
#
# `WEBAUTHN_RP_ID` is the domain the credential belongs to and `WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN`
# the exact origin the ceremony must come from. Get either wrong and every sign
# in fails the same way — the browser refuses before the API is even asked — so
# they are two lines rather than one derived from the other.
#
# **A passkey is bound to one RP ID and works nowhere else.** That is the point
# of it, and it has consequences worth knowing in advance rather than
# discovering: the production passkey does not work on `localhost`, so local
# development needs its own enrolment; every preview deployment has its own
# hostname and will not accept the production passkey; and moving the site to
# another domain means re-enrolling every device.
#
# Deployed, these are the Vercel origin the SPA is served from:
#   WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=<project>.vercel.app
#   WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=https://<project>.vercel.app
#
# **Funnel does not touch either of these**, and that is the whole reason the
# phone path was built this way round. The passkey is bound to where the *page*
# comes from, and under Funnel the page still comes from Vercel; only the API
# call underneath it changes who can route to it. Every already-enrolled device
# keeps working, with no re-enrolment.
#
# Serving the SPA from the `ts.net` host instead — a second Funnel handler on
# `/` with the API moved under `/api` — would make these
# `pc.<tailnet>.ts.net` / `https://pc.<tailnet>.ts.net`, and **every existing
# passkey would be refused from the first sign-in after the change**, because a
# credential enrolled under one RP ID is not offered under another. That is a
# full re-enrolment of every device from the PC's own keyboard
# (`node scripts/api.mjs enroll`), including the PC's, and it is a one-way door
# in practice: moving back refuses the passkeys enrolled while it was moved.
WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=localhost
WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=http://localhost:5173

# How many Google Flights requests one collection day may spend. Unset — the
# default — means there is no ceiling at all, which is deliberate: the endpoint
# is unmetered, the real limit is how much traffic one address can send before
# it stops being answered, and that number is unknown and deliberately
# unprobed. What bounds a pass is the three-second gap between requests and the
# one-pass-at-a-time lock, which bound the rate rather than a total. Set a
# number here to put the whole ceiling back; `0`, `off` or `none` say the
# default out loud for an environment that would rather keep the line.
# FARES_DAILY_REQUEST_BUDGET=off

# Leave the official SKY booking-API fallback disabled unless the collector
# should supplement Google H2 nulls with tax-inclusive SKY totals.
SKY_OFFICIAL_LOOKUP_ENABLED=false
